how did bullet time go from a filmmaking  phrase to a core aspect of the matrix  resurrections how much of a doors fan do  you need to be to understand the new  film these are the lines in matrix  resurrections that mean more than you  realize  early in the film neo's co-worker jude  tells him quote i'm a geek raised by  machines he's ostensibly talking about  how he grew up playing video games but  it's clear that a line this blatant  carries some extra meaning part of this  line is smack in the face obvious he's a  dude who lives in the matrix he was  literally raised by machines what's less  obvious however is how much it  foreshadows jude's true nature when bugs  meets neo she tells him jude is actually  working with the agents sure enough jude  is on the lookout for neo which is more  than a good enough reason for neo to  leave with bugs he wasn't just raised by  machines as a human but as a potential  threat it's also possible that jude's  name means more than it seems much of  the matrix series can be read as a  christ allegory as such it's not a  stretch to assume that jude the name of  a man who betrays neo is derived from  judas  in the first act of resurrections  jonathan graf's smith tells neo that  their company has been commissioned to  make a fourth matrix game before  delivering one of the most meta-textual  lines of the film that's the thing about  stories they never really end  resurrections is a sequel to a long  dormant series both in real life and as  a game in universe so of course the  story never ended the line is true  literally and meta textually given that  resurrections is in large part a matrix  movie about making matrix movies what's  easier to overlook especially if you  haven't seen the sequels in a while is  that it stands in direct opposition to  what we heard in the third movie in  revolutions neo is told by the oracle  that everything that has a beginning has  an end after the oracle is absorbed by  smith the agent himself delivers the  line which clues neo in on the sacrifice  he needs to make to save both the  machine world and the human world of  zion  multiple characters throughout  resurrections use the phrase bullet time  some of the game developers use it to  describe the old matrix games the  analyst uses it to describe technology  and techniques he innovated to get the  upper hand over neo most fans will  recognize bullet time as the terminology  long used to describe the franchise's  slo-mo fight scenes  you move like they do  i've never seen anyone move that fast so  this just seems like an inside joke  right well it isn't not that but here's  what's easy to forget the term has never  been used in a matrix movie before now a  big part of this is the meta textual  nature of the movie much of  resurrections is quite literally about  making a matrix property there are some  curious implications attached to making  a filmmaking term canon  when neo starts to get back into the  flow of things with bugs and her crew  they refer to neo's mojo rising younger  fans or at least those not familiar with  classic rock might not recognize the  reference here rock fans however will  recognize mojo rising as a version of mr  mojo ryzen a nickname used by jim  morrison of the doors jim morrison whose  lyrics were often trippy and  consciousness pushing also went by mr  mojo ryzen an anagram for his name this  can most notably be heard in the song la  woman where he repeats it frequently the  doors also took their name from the  doors of perception a book noted author  aldous huxley wrote about his mescaline  trips it shouldn't be a huge jump to see  how a story about heightened reality and  changing perspective might want to name  drop morrison  free  your mind  later in the movie niobe tells neo my  friends let me make up my own choices  while mulling over the mission to save  trinity whether it contradicts or  reinforces her beliefs from reloaded and  revolutions is like many things in the  matrix movies a matter of perspective  for most of resurrections niobe is stuck  in her ways she refuses to confront the  machines she's not particularly  interested in liberating more humans and  she's perfectly content just running  defense in her own city where this gets  complicated is how it reflects on her  actions and revolutions in revolutions  niobe gives neo command of the logo so  he can visit machine city it's a choice  that she makes of her own free will the  oracle's message to naobi was simply  that she would have to choose whether or  not to help neo naobi's line in  resurrections pairs well with another  observation she makes that she never  believed in the one that might be true  but she may be misremembering the other  half of the sentiment that her younger  counterpart delivered  you've never believed in the one  i still don't then why are you doing  this  i believe in him  unfortunately by the time we see her  again in resurrections it seems that  niobe no longer believes in neo either  after neo reunites with sati she finally  explains how neo and trinity were  revived part of it is a personal  connection her father created the  resurrection pods that brought neo and  trinity back from the dead it's a neat  little bit of continuity and part of it  is easy to miss if you forgot her  father's full title what what was it  again  i am the power plant systems manager for  recycling operations  back in revolutions neo met sati and her  parents at the mobile avenue station  tati's father ramacandra introduces  himself we don't actually see his work  in action but it's quite likely that  recycling involves turning dead humans  into the goo that gets recycled back  into nutrients for the trapped humans as  such it makes perfect narrative sense  that a recycling manager would be in  charge of the titular resurrection as  neo would say  check out one of our newest videos right  here plus even more looper videos about  your favorite movies are coming soon  sub